It was first released as GB 18030-2000, revised for the first time in 2005; this is the second revision.
GB 18030-2022
Information Expansion Technique "Chinese Encoding Character Set"
1. This document specifies Chinese graphic characters for information technology and the hexadecimal representation of binary encoding.
This document applies to the processing, exchange, storage, transmission, display, input, and output of information containing Chinese characters and other graphical and symbolic characters.
This document applies to technical products with the function of information processing and exchange of Chinese and other text graphics characters, including but not limited to software products represented by input methods, optical character recognition (OCR), editing and proofreading, machine translation, speech synthesis, text transcription, and intelligent writing, as well as portable products represented by computers, communication terminal devices, e-readers, and learning machines.
2 Regulatory Referenced Documents
The content of the following documents is indispensable to this document through normative references.
Among them, referenced documents with dates noted apply only to the version corresponding to that date; referenced documents without dates noted apply the latest edition (including all amendments) to this document.
GB/T 2312-1980 Information Exchange Chinese Character Encoding Character Set Basic Set
GB/T 11383-1989 Information Processing - Eight-Bit Code Structure and Encoding Rules for Information Exchange
GB/T 13000 Information Technology - Universal Character Set (UCS)
3 Terms and Definitions
The following terms and definitions apply to this document.
3.1 Character
An element in a collection of elements used for organizing, controlling, or representing data.
3.2 Coded Character
Characters (3.1) and their encoding.
3.3 User-Defined Private Use Area
Users of products that comply with this document can specify their own regions.
3.4 Vocabulary Repertoire
A set of characters specified by a coding character set (3.2).
3.5 Reserved Zone
Reserve for the future the area specified in this document.
GB 18030-2022
4 Dictionary
4.1 Overview
The characters included in this document are encoded in single-byte, double-byte, or quadruple-byte formats.
The single-byte section includes all 128 characters from 0x00 to 0x7F in GB/T 11383-1989.
4.3 Double-Byte Part
The double-byte section includes all graphic characters from GB/T 2312-1980, the CJK Unified Hanzi from GB/T 13000, as well as some graphic characters.
Characters in the double-byte section shall be in accordance with the provisions of Appendix A.
Among them, the graphical representation, code position, and function of the ideographic descriptor should comply with the provisions of Appendix B.
Note: GB/T 13000 unifies the encoding of Chinese characters used in China, Japan, South Korea, Vietnam, and other countries and regions.
Chinese characters with unique abstract shapes are assigned separate code positions; Chinese characters with the same abstract shape but from different sources are assigned shared code positions.
CJK Unified Ideographs refer to the encoded Chinese characters, where CJK stands for China, Japan, and Korea.
4.4 Four-Byte Section
The four-byte section includes 66 CJK Unified Ideographs from GB/T 13000 (9FA6 to 9FEF, excluding the 8 characters from 9FB4 to 9FBB), CJK Unified Ideographs Extension A, CJK Unified Ideographs Extension B, CJK Unified Ideographs Extension C, CJK Unified Ideographs Extension D, CJK Unified Ideographs Extension E, CJK Unified Ideographs Extension F, and characters of minority languages that have already been encoded in GB/T 13000.
The characters in the four-byte portion shall be in accordance with the provisions of Appendix C.
5 Overall Structure
The numbers in the text that are prefixed with 0x are in hexadecimal format, while those without the 0x prefix are in decimal format.
All numerical representations in the appendix are in hexadecimal for codes, and other numbers are in decimal.
The single-byte part uses the encoding structure of GB/T 11383-1989, using code positions from 0x00 to 0x7F.
The double-byte area uses two eight-bit binary strings to represent a character, with the first byte ranging from 0x81 to 0xFE, and the second byte ranging from 0x40 to 0x7E and 0x80 to 0xFE.
The four-byte part uses 0x30-0x39 as a suffix for the extension of the double-byte encoding, which is not adopted in GB/11383-1989, with an encoding range of 0x81308130 to 0xFE39FE39.
The first byte of a four-byte character is encoded in the range 0x81 to 0xFE; the second byte is encoded in the range 0x30 to 0x39; the third byte is encoded in the range 0x81 to 0xFE; and the fourth byte is encoded in the range 0x30 to 0x39. That is: 0x81308130 to 0x81308139; 0x81308230 to 0x81308239; 0x8130FEFE30 to 0x8130FE39; 0x81318130 to 0x81318139; 0xFE39FE30 to 0xFE39FE39.
