The C and C++ compilers are integrated. They each use one or more of the four steps to process the input files: preprocessing, compilation, assembly, and linking.
The file extension identifies the language of the source file, but for the compiler, the file extension controls the default settings:
GCC treats the preprocessed file (.i) as a C file and sets the C format for linking.
g++ treats the preprocessed file (.i) as a C++ file and sets up C++-style linking.
The file extension indicates the language type and the subsequent operations:
