-funsigned-char defines char as an unsigned type, like unsigned char.
Each machine has its own default char type. It could be either unsigned char or signed char.
In an ideal scenario, when using data-dependent symbolism, a portable program should always use signed char or unsigned char.
However, many programs have been written to use simple char types, and whether this is a signed or unsigned type depends on the target machine for which the program is written.
This option and its opposite make the program work with the corresponding default values.
The type char should always be explicitly defined as either a signed char or an unsigned char, even if its behavior is identical to one of them.
If the 'signed' or 'unsigned' modifier is not explicitly declared, these options are used to define signed bitfields or unsigned bitfields.
