(Deutsche Welle) It has been several days since an earthquake struck parts of Turkey and Syria, and the hope of finding survivors has become increasingly slim. However, a family of five rescued on Saturday brought immense comfort to rescue workers.
The five-member family, trapped under the ruins for more than five days in the heavily affected Nurlaï town in Gaziantep province, was successfully rescued according to a report by AFP.
The search and rescue personnel first pulled out the mother and daughter from the pile of ruins, then found the father, but he insisted on saving his son and the other daughter first.
After they were all rescued, cheers echoed through the crowd.
In southern Turkey's city of Kahramanmaraş, a 16-year-old boy and a 70-year-old elderly woman were also successfully rescued under harsh cold weather conditions.
What day is today?
The 129-hour trapped boy, who was buried under the ruins, asked the search and rescue personnel who saved him when he was brought back to the surface.
A man from Kahramanmaraş is holding the cat he just rescued.
The miraculous successful rescue case brought a glimmer of hope to the already desperate search and rescue efforts, yet at the same time, the number of victims was continuously rising.
According to official statistics, the earthquake on Monday has already caused more than 20,000 deaths in Turkey, with the death toll in neighboring Syria estimated to exceed 3,500.
Although experts have stated that people can survive for more than a week after being trapped under the ruins, the hope of finding more survivors has become increasingly渺茫.
Rescue personnel are still using thermal imaging cameras to search for signs of life beneath the ruins.
At the same time, the focus of the rescue efforts began to shift towards helping the survivors in urgent need of assistance.
United Nations officials: A disaster "unprecedented in a century" in the region
United Nations Under-Secretary-General for Humanitarian Affairs and Emergency Relief Coordinator Martin Griffiths said at a press conference in Turkey that this earthquake is "the worst disaster in the region in a century."
He also praised Turkey's response to the disaster as "unusual".
He also told Reuters that he hopes to provide aid to areas controlled by the Syrian government and opposition groups, but also noted that the matter is "still unclear."
The United Nations has pledged an additional $25 million in aid donations to the people affected by the earthquake in Syria.
Earlier this week, the United Nations announced a funding of 25 million dollars for disaster relief in Turkey and Syria.
The most destructive earthquake in the world in the past 60 years
1960 Chilean Earthquake
The afternoon of May 22, 1960, local time, a earthquake of magnitude 9.5 on the moment magnitude scale was recorded in Valdivia, Chile, making it the largest earthquake ever observed in human history.
The entire city of Valdivia turned into ruins within just ten minutes.
A massive tsunami, triggered by an earthquake, struck the Chilean coastline, with wave heights reaching 25 meters.
The tsunami reached distant Japan and the Philippines.
The number of casualties and economic losses caused by such a widespread disaster cannot be precisely determined.
The most destructive earthquake in the world over the past 60 years
Alaska Earthquake (1964)
March 27, 1964 was a Friday, which was also Good Friday.
On this day, Alaska, USA experienced the largest earthquake in North American history, with a moment magnitude of 9.2.
Until 2006, the Alaskan earthquake was still the third largest earthquake in recorded history.
The fault between the Pacific Plate and the North American Plate broke near College Inlet in Prince William Sound during the earthquake.
The earthquake lasted from 3 to 5 minutes in most areas.
The sea bed movement triggered a massive tsunami with a height of 67 meters.
Because it was a holiday that day, schools and many companies were closed.
This earthquake ultimately resulted in 139 deaths.
Of the 9 people, 9 died directly from the earthquake, and the other 130 died from the tsunami caused by the earthquake.
The most destructive earthquake in the world over the past 60 years
Tangshan Earthquake (1976)
On July 28, 1976, two strong earthquakes with magnitudes of 7 or higher occurred in Tangshan City, Fengnan County, Hebei Province, China within 16 hours.
The hypocenter is 23 kilometers above the ground.
Tianjin's entire transportation, communication, water supply, and power supply were cut off, and the city was reduced to ruins in an instant.
The capital Beijing, 140 kilometers away, also experienced strong shaking.
The official statistics show that, including Beijing and Tianjin, the earthquake caused 242,769 deaths and 164,851 people to be seriously injured in all the affected areas.
Taiyuan Earthquake became the second deadliest earthquake in China with recorded history, ranking behind the 1556 Jiangli Earthquake.
However, overseas scientists estimate that this earthquake may cause at least 650,000 deaths.
The most destructive earthquake in the world over the past 60 years
2004 Indian Ocean Earthquake
On December 26, 2004, an earthquake occurred 160 kilometers from the western coast of Aceh province on the island of Sumatra, Indonesia, with a hypocenter depth of 30 kilometers and a moment magnitude of 9.1 to 9.3.
An earthquake triggered a massive tsunami with wave heights reaching 15 to 30 meters.
The Pacific coastal region is affected by 14 countries in total, with the most severely affected areas including Aceh Province in Indonesia, the Phuket and Krabi tourist areas in Thailand, Sri Lanka, and the Tamil Nadu region in India.
It was during the peak travel season of Christmas, when the affected area was crowded with a large number of tourists and local residents. This led to many tourists enjoying their holidays on the beach and local residents working by the sea being swept under by a tsunami, resulting in many people becoming missing due to the earthquake.
The estimated number of victims and missing persons is at least 300,000.
The most destructive earthquake in the world over the past 60 years
Wenchuan Earthquake (2008)
On May 12, 2008, a great earthquake occurred in the Wenchuan area, Sichuan Province, China.
According to data from the China Earthquake Administration, the surface wave magnitude of this earthquake reached 8.2, and the moment magnitude reached 8.3, with the affected areas exceeding 100,000 square kilometers.
As of September 25, 2008, the earthquake caused a total of 69,227 deaths, 17,923 missing persons, 374,643 people injured at various degrees, and 19.93 million people homeless. The total number of affected people reached 46.256 million.
It is the most destructive earthquake since the founding of the People's Republic of China, and also the most devastating one after the Tangshan Earthquake.
In March 2009, the State Council of China announced that May 12th every year would be designated as Disaster Prevention and Reduction Day to strengthen public education and attention to disaster prevention and reduction work.
After that, governments in many places in China and primary and secondary schools hold earthquake drills on this day every year.
Primary and secondary schools are also beginning to emphasize the promotion of earthquake and disaster prevention education.
The most destructive earthquake that has occurred worldwide in the past 60 years
2010 Haitian earthquake
On January 12, 2010, a 7.0 magnitude earthquake struck Haiti, a Caribbean island nation.
The epicenter is located 25 kilometers to the south-west of Port-au-Prince, the capital of Haiti, and the focus is about 13 kilometers below the surface.
The International Red Cross estimates that the population affected by the earthquake is approximately three million.
The Haitian government has announced that the number of confirmed deaths exceeds 200,000.
In some areas within Haiti, as many as 90% of the houses were destroyed in the earthquake.
Although the international community has provided substantial aid and support, this poor country with overpopulation and rampant corruption has never recovered from this disaster.
The most destructive earthquake that has occurred worldwide in the past 60 years
2011 Japanese Northeast Earthquake
On March 11, 2011, a large reverse fault earthquake with a magnitude of 9.0 occurred off the coast of the northeastern region of Japan.
The epicenter was located in the Pacific Ocean east of the capital of Miyagi Prefecture, Sendai City, with a hypocenter depth of 24 kilometers, and it triggered a massive tsunami with a maximum run-up height of 40.1 meters.
This earthquake is the first one with a magnitude exceeding 9 recorded in Japan's history.
About 22,000 people died in the disaster, and approximately 400,000 buildings were completely destroyed or collapsed.
