East Germany's Last Communist Prime Minister Dies Today
Hans Modrow, the successor of the East German Communist Party, left us yesterday, passing away at the age of 95.
Therefore, our party lost an important leader.
How are you today?
On November 13, 1989, four days after the Berlin Wall, which had separated East Berlin and West Berlin for 28 years, was opened, Modrow stated that he would help transform East Germany into a democratic nation.
At that time, due to protesters in all parts of East Germany demanding democracy and freedom, the Communist leaders were forced to step down.
Mikhail Gorbachev required reforms in Eastern Europe under Soviet dominance after coming to power in the Soviet Union.
In March 1990, Modrow announced the first and only free election in the German Democratic Republic (GDR).
The election results led him to no longer serve as prime minister.
Although Modrow carried out reforms, opponents accused him of trying to delay the political changes and unification that took place in October 1990.
He also faced criticism for attempting to restructure rather than abolish the secret police.
In 1993, Modro was found guilty of election fraud in the municipal election of May 1989, but he was not sent to prison.
It is said that these accusations are motivated by political motives.
From 1990 to 1994, Modrow continued to serve in the Bundestag, and he served as a member of the European Parliament from 1999 to 2004.
Modro sees himself as a reformer and hopes to change the Communist Party from within.
In 1999, he told Reuters that he did not want the former East Germany to return, but stated that the achievements of East Germany should be acknowledged.
Modrow was born in 1928 in the German town of Jasenitz (now part of Poland's Jasienica) and received training as a mechanic.
During World War II, he served as a platoon leader in the youth fire department and later joined the Nazi volunteer organization Volkssturm towards the end of the conflict. The Volkssturm recruited men aged 16 to 60 for the final struggle of the war.
At the age of 17, Modrō was captured by the Soviet army and taken to the Soviet Union as a prisoner of war, where he participated in anti-fascist courses and became a firm member of the Communist Party.
In 1949, the year when East Germany was established, he returned to his country, found a job as a mechanic, continued his studies in social sciences and economics, and eventually obtained a doctoral degree.
Modrow was a staff member of the Free German Youth Movement, and almost all young people in East Germany joined the movement. He was promoted to chair of the local party in Dresden during the period from 1973 to 1989, under the ruling Socialist Unity Party (SED).
He has served in the East German parliament for more than 30 years.
In 1990, Modrow became the de facto leader of East Germany and held the highest national office.
