What exactly was the "object" that fell in the skies of Alaska?
Confusing and uncertain, with many guesses and speculations
The U.S. military again used its most advanced stealth jet, the F-22, in the afternoon on Friday (February 10) to shoot down an unidentified object flying over Alaska.
The location where the aircraft was shot down is ten miles off the Alaskan coast.
This is the second time the U.S. military has downed an aerial object within less than a week.
Last Saturday, a spy balloon from China was destroyed near the coast of South Carolina after drifting in U.S. airspace for several days.
The Pentagon said that the second object first entered U.S. airspace on Thursday night, moving over the northeast of Alaska and tracked by the U.S. military. It flew at an altitude of 12,000 meters (40,000 feet) and was about the size of a small car, much smaller than the previously downed Chinese spy balloon.
The Pentagon still believes there is no evidence currently indicating that the object is controllable.
White House National Security Council strategic communication coordinator John Kirby said on Friday that although the specific appearance of the object flying at high altitude is still unclear, it "poses a reasonable threat to the safety of commercial aircraft."
Kobe stated that this object is different from the spy balloon found within the United States; it does not carry a "payload."
Kobe said, "Calling it an 'object' is the best description we can currently make."
Biden listened to the advice of the Pentagon before ordering the missile strike, and he made the decision "very carefully,"科比补充说。
The official description of this object in the United States is roughly as follows: it is about the size of a car, has no steering wheel, no suspension system, and flies at an altitude of around ten thousand meters.
But this information still cannot answer basic questions, such as what this object is, which country it belongs to, and what task it is performing.
Many analyses and speculations online currently suggest that this may be another Chinese balloon.
It may be from China, just like the spy balloon that invaded American airspace and the Chinese balloon floating over Latin America, both of which are carrying out certain tasks in different locations.
Some people have also linked this "object" in Alaska to a "flying saucer" (UFO).
Reports from American Newsweek cited a tweet from former U.S. Representative Peter Meijer on Friday, who said, "I really hope this 'unidentified flying object' is a spy balloon, not a legitimate UFO."
"The irony of confusing an alien spacecraft with a weather balloon is surprising, but it's not worth provoking an interstellar conflict," said Meijer.
The tweet was posted by Mei Jie shortly after Kobe confirmed that the object in Alaska had been shot down.
The Newsweek said that it is not just Major who suspects this object is a UFO.
Republican congressman Tim Burchett from Tennessee criticized the government's practice of covering up intelligence last month and demanded that the National Intelligence Director's Office (DNI) release a report on "unidentified flying objects."
