I read it a long time ago, and I happened to see it. Let me write down some thoughts based on my memory.
This book is more of a collection of fragments about love in life, rather than a short story collection.
The tone of this book is extremely plain, like plain water, and the language is pursued to the extreme of simplicity. The plot is as quiet and plain as the streets on a summer afternoon.
Each short story is a snapshot of a brief moment in everyday life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally collected together, it is named "What Are We Talking About When We Talk About Love?"
I think this collection of stories and its title resemble a form of performance art. The author wants to convey that love is not merely the tragic and romantic scenes on stage, the eternal vows in urban legends, nor just the intense passion and entanglement of being deeply in love. Love is not just these things, and it should not be represented by them. Love is also the ordinary and uneventful life, the monotonous days repeating day after day, and the helplessness in life, the escape from reality, and the uncertainty about the future.
When you read this book, you may imagine the arguing couple next to you, the dull marriages of people you know, and unsatisfying relationships.
This is exactly why I don't recommend reading this book. From an aesthetic perspective, the writing and plot are dull and uninteresting. Unless your life is full of dramatic upheavals or as sweet as living in a candy castle, the feeling you get while reading it will be one of indifference and boredom.
In terms of intellectual depth, the daily plot does not impress the eye or expand one's thinking and perspective (a point different from Maupassant's "The Tale of the Heir"). The brief scenes do not explore any issues, such as what kind of mistakes led to the failure of love?
What are the reasons for the breakdown of the main character's marriage?
So I don't recommend reading this book; whether you read it or not, it doesn't really make much difference.
The author's ideas are more expressed through techniques and genres rather than the plot, which is also the reason why the reading experience of this book is somewhat poor.
