The first factor that determines a person's job satisfaction is not the level of salary, but whether they are given enough attention and recognition in their work.
According to Maslow's hierarchy of needs theory, after basic survival needs are met, the most urgent need is the satisfaction of psychological needs—feeling understood, appreciated, and recognized.
Indeed, for the Chinese people, most of them feel "I love you but can't say it out loud." Even if they have love, gratitude, and admiration, they tend to keep these feelings hidden in their hearts and never express them verbally.
The author elaborates on five languages of appreciation in this book: words of affirmation, meaningful moments, acts of service, thoughtful gifts, and physical touch.
What is important is not that you know exactly what the five languages of appreciation are, but that you can genuinely use these five languages to appreciate and be grateful to people around you, whether they are family, friends, or colleagues.
Actually, the closer the relationship, the more we need to appreciate and be grateful, which will make our lives more beautiful.
Learning to appreciate can help companies retain talent to a certain extent and reduce turnover rates.
